                                   qSHA256

           What would it actually cost a quantum computer to compute
                                  SHA-256?


qSHA256 builds SHA-256 out of reversible quantum circuits, executes them to
prove they are correct, measures what they cost, and searches for cheaper ones.

The usual answer to "how does SHA-256 fare against quantum computers" is 2^128,
Grover's query count for a 256-bit preimage. That number says nothing about what
a query costs. This project builds the circuit underneath it: the qubits, the
reversible arithmetic, the ancillas, the uncomputation, the Toffoli and T
counts, and the two-full-SHA-256-evaluations that one Grover query actually is.

    NOTE  This is a research and educational project, not a cryptography
          library. It does not hash anything faster than hashlib, provides
          no security guarantees, and has never run on quantum hardware.
          It does not break SHA-256 and finds no weakness in it.

Licensed under Apache-2.0. See the LICENSE file.


THE HEADLINE RESULT
===============================================================================

A reversible circuit for the full 64-round, 32-bit SHA-256 compression function
is constructed, executed, and verified against hashlib:

    $ qsha256 validate
    ...
    Layer 4 - full 64-round SHA-256
      [PASS] sha256 compression, 64 rounds, forward == classical
      [PASS] sha256 compression, 64 rounds, garbage-free == classical
      [PASS] 32-bit 64-round circuit digest == hashlib.sha256(b'abc')

    Layer 5 - optimization passes
      [PASS] phase folding preserves the unitary exactly
      [PASS] Gidney temporary-AND circuits == classical (preconditions checked)
    ...
    17/17 checks passed, 6,129 cases

This is not a scaled-down proxy. Every SHA-256 compute circuit here is a
permutation circuit, using only X, CNOT and Toffoli, so it can be executed
exactly on computational basis states in time linear in the gate count, with no
statevector required. A 1,057-qubit circuit that no simulator on Earth could
handle in superposition runs in under a second on basis states, and produces
ba7816bf...15ad for b"abc".


CURRENT RESOURCE RESULTS
===============================================================================

All figures are measured from constructed circuits and regenerated by
"python scripts/generate_benchmarks.py". Full tables live in
benchmarks/results/latest.md.


Scaling with round count
------------------------

Default architecture (CDKM adder, rolling schedule, serial round layout),
forward evaluation only:

    Rounds   Qubits      Gates    Toffoli      Depth    T-count
    ------   ------      -----    -------      -----    -------
         1    1,057      4,186      1,088      2,426      7,616
         2    1,057      6,582      1,664      3,566     11,648
         4    1,057     11,398      2,816      5,846     19,712
         8    1,057     20,998      5,120     10,406     35,840
        16    1,057     40,194      9,728     19,526     68,096
        32    1,057     93,528     22,016     45,558    154,112
        64    1,057    200,162     46,592     97,622    326,144

T-counts are measured from a circuit actually transpiled into Clifford+T, and
match the analytical "7 x Toffoli" model exactly, which anchors the model to a
real compiler.

The cost is not linear in rounds: the message schedule does not begin expanding
until round 16, because rounds 0 to 15 consume the message block directly.


Post-construction optimization
------------------------------

Two optimizations below the architectural level. Gidney temporary ANDs replace
every adder Toffoli with a 4-T compute and a measurement-based, T-free
uncomputation. Phase folding merges T gates acting on the same GF(2) linear
function, which is the core idea of T-par.

                          Qubits  Toffoli   and_g   T-count  vs base  NC depth
                          ------  -------   -----   -------  -------  --------
    cdkm (baseline)        1,057   46,592       0   326,144        -    38,528
    cdkm + phase folding   1,057        0       0   181,568   -44.3%   149,312
    gidney temporary AND   1,087    8,192  18,600   131,744   -59.6%    18,791
    gidney + phase fold    1,087        0  18,600   107,168   -67.1%    19,559

    NC depth = non-Clifford depth: Toffolis, T gates and AND-computes together.

Read the depth column alongside the T-count. Phase folding cuts T-count sharply
but raises non-Clifford depth, because merging phases onto the first point where
each linear function is live serialises them. The Gidney adder improves both at
once, but only on hardware with mid-circuit measurement and classical
feedforward, an assumption the other designs do not make.

A single 32-bit addition goes from 448 T (CDKM) to 124 T (Gidney).


The three circuits that matter
------------------------------

                                Qubits   Toffoli    T-count      Depth
                                ------   -------    -------      -----
    forward compression          1,057    46,592    326,144     97,622
    garbage-free (uncomputed)    1,313    92,672    648,704    193,874
    preimage oracle              1,535    94,202    659,414    195,280

One Grover query costs 2.02x a forward SHA-256 evaluation. That is measured, not
assumed. It is a garbage-free forward hash, a 256-bit digest comparison, and a
full inverse hash.


Where the cost goes
-------------------

    Component                 Toffoli        %
    ---------                 -------    -----
    T1 accumulation            20,480    44.0%
    T2 accumulation            12,288    26.4%
    message schedule            9,216    19.8%
    d += T1                     4,096     8.8%
    chaining addition             512     1.1%
    H -> working copy               0     0.0%

Components are disjoint and sum exactly to the circuit total. Modular addition
dominates. The sigma functions cost zero Toffolis, because rotations and shifts
are wire relabellings rather than gates.


COMPARISON WITH PUBLISHED WORK
===============================================================================

Against Amy, Di Matteo, Gheorghiu, Mosca, Parent and Schanck (SAC 2016,
arXiv:1603.09383), Table 1. Figures are transcribed with per-metric
comparability verdicts, so a Toffoli-level depth is never silently divided by a
Clifford+T depth.

Their T-par-optimized circuit reports 228,992 T at 2,402 logical qubits.

    qSHA256 design        Qubits   T-count  vs published  Same machine model?
    --------------        ------   -------  ------------  -------------------
    default (as built)     1,057   326,144        +42.4%  yes, unitary
    + phase folding        1,057   181,568        -20.7%  yes, unitary
    + Gidney ANDs          1,087   131,744        -42.5%  no, needs feedforward
    + Gidney and folding   1,087   107,168        -53.2%  no, needs feedforward

The like-for-like comparison is the "+ phase folding" row. Both are unitary
Clifford+T circuits with phase-polynomial optimization applied, so it is a fair
contest: qSHA256 reaches a 20.7% lower T-count at 44% of the qubits.

The Gidney rows go further, but they assume mid-circuit measurement and
feedforward, a capability the 2016 circuit did not assume, since Gidney's
construction postdates it by two years. That is a comparison between machine
models, not between two circuits for the same machine, and the leaderboard says
so wherever it appears.

Against their pre-T-par circuit the default design already wins on every
comparable metric: 56% fewer qubits, 19% fewer T gates, 10% lower T-depth.


AUTOMATED DESIGN SEARCH
===============================================================================

qSHA256 does not ship one circuit. It ships a space of provably-correct circuits
and searches it.

    $ qsha256 search --spec toy4 --rounds 8

    224 designs evaluated in 106s; 16 on the Pareto front over
    qubits, t_count, non_clifford_depth

    gidney/load/rolling/serial/folded:  t_count -72.4%, nc_depth -45.0%
                                            at the cost of qubits +2.4%
    gidney/load/rolling/wide:           t_count -64.9%, nc_depth -69.2%
                                            at the cost of qubits +21.2%
    cdkm/load/rolling/serial/rewritten: t_count -20.7%, nc_depth -24.4%
    cdkm/vbe_const/rolling/serial/folded: t_count -54.7%
                                            at the cost of nc_depth +198.4%
    qft/load/rolling/wide:              nc_depth -97.4%
                                            at the cost of t_count +10627.0%

Every design is functionally verified against the classical reference before its
numbers are allowed into a report. All 216 basis-simulable designs pass. The 8
QFT-adder designs are labelled UNSUPPORTED rather than silently trusted.

The objective is non-Clifford depth, not Toffoli depth. A phase-folded circuit
contains no Toffolis at all, so scoring it on Toffoli depth would read zero and
look like a free win. Counting Toffolis, T gates and AND-computes together is
the one depth measure that stays meaningful across all three representations.

Four findings worth stating:

  1. Gidney temporary ANDs are the single biggest lever. A 32-bit addition
     drops from 448 T to 124, and the full compression from 326,144 T to
     131,744, while halving non-Clifford depth, because the uncomputation is
     Clifford.

  2. The "wide" round layout cuts depth 25% at identical T-count, for 128 extra
     qubits. That is the best trade available without changing the gate set.

  3. The QFT adder is a trap. It has by far the fewest Toffolis (8,192 against
     46,592) and by far the worst T-count (301 million), because arbitrary-angle
     rotations must be synthesised from Clifford+T. A Toffoli count is not a
     fault-tolerant cost.

  4. Carry-save addition loses. On paper it should win, since three
     constant-depth CSA layers beat four ripple adders. Reversible
     uncomputation doubles the tree and it comes out worse: 58,496 Toffolis
     against 46,592. A negative result, reported as one.

The gate-level rewriter also rediscovers a human optimization. Constant folding
specialises the generic adder in const_add="load" down to exactly the circuit
that const_add="vbe_const" hard-codes by hand. Both converge to the same Toffoli
count, and that is asserted as a test.

Every optimization is verified, not assumed. Phase folding is checked to
preserve the unitary exactly, including global phase, on hundreds of random
circuits. The Gidney ANDs are simulated in a strict mode that asserts every
and_g target is |0> and every and_g_dg target holds exactly x AND y, since
violating either is a silent correctness trap.


GROVER, WITHOUT THE HYPE
===============================================================================

    $ qsha256 grover-demo     # actually runs, on a toy
    $ qsha256 oracle          # full-scale oracle cost + extrapolation

The toy demo executes real amplitude amplification on a ~15-qubit reduced hash,
raising a planted solution from probability 0.0625 to 0.9613. It is labelled a
toy everywhere it appears.

For real SHA-256, the oracle is constructed and measured, then combined with the
analytic query count and labelled EXTRAPOLATED. Nothing is simulated.

Three things the plain 2^128 story omits, all of which the reports state:

  1. A query is not a hash evaluation. Measured at 2.02x here.
  2. Grover barely parallelises. m machines buy sqrt(m), not m.
  3. Depth limits bite. 2^128 iterations is inherently serial, which is why
     NIST evaluates such attacks under an explicit MAXDEPTH cap.

See docs/grover.md.


INSTALL AND USE
===============================================================================

    git clone https://github.com/icedmoca/qSHA256.git
    cd qSHA256
    python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
    pip install -e ".[dev]"

    qsha256 validate                     # verify circuits against the model
    qsha256 analyze --rounds 64          # measure logical resources
    qsha256 benchmark                    # scaling table
    qsha256 search --spec toy4           # Pareto search over the design space
    qsha256 oracle                       # preimage oracle + Grover cost
    qsha256 physical --model optimistic  # fault-tolerant estimate
    qsha256 leaderboard                  # compare against published circuits
    qsha256 grover-demo                  # run the toy Grover search

    qsha256 analyze --rounds 64 --adder gidney --phase-fold   # lowest T-count

As a library:

    from qsha256 import SHA256
    from qsha256.quantum.sha256.compression import build_compression
    from qsha256.quantum.resources import analyze

    comp = build_compression(SHA256, rounds=64)
    print(analyze(comp, spec=SHA256, rounds=64))


HOW THE NUMBERS ARE LABELLED
===============================================================================

Every figure carries its provenance, because "SHA-256 needs N T gates" means
something different under each:

    MEASURED              counted off a constructed circuit
    TRANSPILED            measured after a compiler rewrote it into Clifford+T
    ANALYTICAL            derived from measured counts via a documented model
    EXTRAPOLATED          projected beyond what was built, with the rule shown
    ASSUMPTION-DEPENDENT  needs a hardware/error-correction model to mean
                          anything

qSHA256 never equates logical with physical qubits, never equates circuit
construction with execution, and never presents a compiler-dependent number as a
physical constant. There is no single "T-count of a Toffoli". There are three
documented models, and every report says which one it used.


REPOSITORY LAYOUT
===============================================================================

    qsha256/
      spec.py             parameterised SHA-256 family; constants derived
                          from prime roots
      classical/          transparent reference model exposing every
                          intermediate
      quantum/
        registers.py      Word views (rotation = free rewiring), recycling
                          ancilla pool
        strategies.py     the design space
        primitives/       XOR, Ch/Maj, AND trees, four adders
                          (CDKM/VBE/QFT/Gidney), Gidney temporary ANDs,
                          carry-save addition
        sha256/           sigma functions, message schedule, round,
                          compression
        oracle/           digest comparison, preimage oracle, toy Grover
        resources/        gate/depth metrics, Clifford+T models, physical
                          estimator, leaderboard, report rendering
        optimization/     rewriting, phase-polynomial folding, equivalence
                          checking, Pareto search, hardware ranking
      validation/         exact basis-state simulator, layered validation
                          suite
      cli.py
    docs/                 reversible computing, architecture, metrics,
                          Grover, limitations
    benchmarks/results/   generated tables (JSON / CSV / Markdown)
    examples/             runnable, self-contained demonstrations
    tests/                360 tests


